/T (Quantitative analysis is initially descriptive. Differences in percentages are reported in percentage points, not treated as causal effects. A measure of 16% versus 32% is a 16-point gap, not proof that a particular teacher or financing policy caused the difference. We do not treat very small differences as statistically significant without reported uncertainty. Ratios presented by federal or state portals are retained as source-provided indicators where the underlying numerator and denominator cannot yet be reproduced. The report separately records mismatches for official clarification.)
/E (Quantitative analysis is initially descriptive. Differences in percentages are reported in percentage points, not treated as causal effects. A measure of 16% versus 32% is a 16-point gap, not proof that a particular teacher or financing policy caused the difference. We do not treat very small differences as statistically significant without reported uncertainty. Ratios presented by federal or state portals are retained as source-provided indicators where the underlying numerator and denominator cannot yet be reproduced. The report separately records mismatches for official clarification.)
