/T (Understanding exclusion also requires studying household constraints rather than presuming disinterest in education. Relevant mechanisms include direct and indirect education costs, seasonal labour, disability-related barriers, travel time, safety and harassment risks, language, early marriage, school quality and whether families trust the curriculum and institution. The strength of each mechanism in Bauchi remains a testable proposition; aggregate attendance rates alone do not reveal which is dominant. Qualitative interviews with caregivers, girls, children with disabilities and non-formal learning leaders should be paired with household and administrative records.)
/E (Understanding exclusion also requires studying household constraints rather than presuming disinterest in education. Relevant mechanisms include direct and indirect education costs, seasonal labour, disability-related barriers, travel time, safety and harassment risks, language, early marriage, school quality and whether families trust the curriculum and institution. The strength of each mechanism in Bauchi remains a testable proposition; aggregate attendance rates alone do not reveal which is dominant. Qualitative interviews with caregivers, girls, children with disabilities and non-formal learning leaders should be paired with household and administrative records.)
/Pg 24 0 R
