/T (The strongest inference is that many pupils who remain in formal schooling are not achieving the assessed minimum competence in particular subjects and grades. Why is less certain. The NALABE contextual analysis relates performance to selected school and family characteristics, but observational associations cannot be read as evidence of causality. Explanations to test include instructional time, language transition, textbook sufficiency, teacher knowledge, class composition, continuity across grades, assessment feedback and school supervision. The fact that learning outcomes are low does not by itself identify whether additional staffing, new facilities, targeted remediation or school leadership support would yield the largest gain.)
/E (The strongest inference is that many pupils who remain in formal schooling are not achieving the assessed minimum competence in particular subjects and grades. Why is less certain. The NALABE contextual analysis relates performance to selected school and family characteristics, but observational associations cannot be read as evidence of causality. Explanations to test include instructional time, language transition, textbook sufficiency, teacher knowledge, class composition, continuity across grades, assessment feedback and school supervision. The fact that learning outcomes are low does not by itself identify whether additional staffing, new facilities, targeted remediation or school leadership support would yield the largest gain.)
/Pg 28 0 R
/K [3]
>>
