/T (A causal diagnosis should distinguish constraints on households, schools, teachers and government administration. Households respond to safety, direct and indirect costs, childcare, transport and trust. Schools respond to usable facilities, capable personnel, materials and management. Teachers respond to deployment conditions, skill, feedback, incentives and school leadership. Government agencies respond to mandates, budget systems, administrative data and accountability. These mechanisms interact: a well-funded classroom with no competent teacher has limited instructional value; a trained teacher with no pupil attendance cannot achieve intended learning outcomes. Identifying the binding constraint requires evidence at the relevant unit, not a statewide generalisation.)
/E (A causal diagnosis should distinguish constraints on households, schools, teachers and government administration. Households respond to safety, direct and indirect costs, childcare, transport and trust. Schools respond to usable facilities, capable personnel, materials and management. Teachers respond to deployment conditions, skill, feedback, incentives and school leadership. Government agencies respond to mandates, budget systems, administrative data and accountability. These mechanisms interact: a well-funded classroom with no competent teacher has limited instructional value; a trained teacher with no pupil attendance cannot achieve intended learning outcomes. Identifying the binding constraint requires evidence at the relevant unit, not a statewide generalisation.)
/Pg 68 0 R
/K [6]
>>
