4728 0 obj
<<
/Title (Functionality varies over time. A toilet marked 'present' may not operate, a borehole may be dry during certain seasons, an electricity connection may fail regularly, and a building may contain classrooms not fit for use. A robust school audit must therefore inspect actual service at a documented moment, record last maintenance, assign a person responsible and use repeat observations where seasonality matters. For every rehabilitation programme the data chain should include site ID, diagnosis, scope, awarded contract, physical verification, operation, handover, maintenance budget and post-handover condition. [E19, E06])
/Dest [36 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4715 0 R
/Prev 4723 0 R
/Next 4729 0 R
>>
endobj
4729 0 obj
<<
/Title (The inclusion dimension cannot be reduced to a disability count. Learners with physical, sensory, cognitive or psychosocial challenges may require changes in access, communications, learning materials and teacher support. Girls require safe and private sanitation and clear safeguarding processes. Remoteness may raise transport and emergency risks for all children. Context-specific hazard mapping, school WASH and accessibility protocols should be undertaken alongside the teacher and enrolment audits, rather than as separate and potentially duplicative exercises.)
/Dest [36 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4715 0 R
/Prev 4728 0 R
/Next 4724 0 R
>>
endobj
4730 0 obj
<<
/Title (The available administrative indicators describe recorded infrastructure and access to basic services, but lack consistently verified information on operability, maintenance, security and accessibility. A school recorded as having water, toilets or classrooms may still face inadequate or unusable service. This distinction is central to targeting investment according to functioning capacity rather than asset counts.)
/Dest [37 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4725 0 R
>>
