/Title (The AGILE programme provides a potentially useful implementation platform. Programme communications in February 2026 reported 12,133 conditional cash-transfer beneficiaries in 100 secondary schools, alongside support for school renovation, life skills and community engagement. Those figures are an output baseline requiring routine verification. A robust evaluation would examine eligible girls and selection rules, approved and successful payments, beneficiary receipt, grievances, attendance persistence, grade transition, completion, wellbeing and learning. It should also account for girls who are eligible but not selected and for differences across schools. The size of a programme's reach is not a direct measure of additional enrolment attributable to the intervention. [E03, E04])
/Dest [39 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 4742 0 R
/Prev 4753 0 R
/Next 4762 0 R
>>
endobj
4762 0 obj
<<
/Title (Earlier GEP3 experience in northern Nigeria, including Bauchi, offers reasons for cautious optimism about combined financial and community support, but its frequently quoted enrolment gains refer to a multi-state programme rather than a Bauchi-specific causal estimate. Comparative randomised research on cash transfers for schooling in Malawi also reveals important design choices: conditions can influence attendance during a programme, while other welfare outcomes may differ. Bauchi should therefore evaluate retention, wellbeing and safety together, not simply report the number of transfers issued. [E14, E15, E16, E17, E18])
/Dest [39 0 R /Fit]
